<article class="env-lemma" id="07VI">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/07VI">Lemma <span data-tag="07VI">15.76.15</span>.</a> Let $A$ be a ring. Let $K \in D(A)$ be perfect. Then $K^\vee = R\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _ A(K, A)$ is a perfect complex and $K \cong (K^\vee )^\vee $. There are functorial isomorphisms </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  L \otimes _ A^\mathbf {L} K^\vee = R\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _ A(K, L) \quad \text{and}\quad H^0(L \otimes _ A^\mathbf {L} K^\vee ) = \mathop{\mathrm{Ext}}\nolimits _ A^0(K, L)  \]
</div>
  <p> for $L \in D(A)$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      We can represent $K$ by a bounded complex $K^\bullet $ of finite projective $A$-modules. By Lemma <a href="/tag/0A66" data-tag="0A66">15.75.2</a> the object $K^\vee $ is represented by the complex $E^\bullet = \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits ^\bullet (K^\bullet , A)$. Note that $E^ n = \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _ A(K^{-n}, A)$ is finite projective as well. Thus $E^\bullet $ is a bounded complex of finite projective modules and we conclude $K^\vee $ is perfect. There is a canonical map 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  K^\bullet = \text{Tot}(\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits ^\bullet (A, A) \otimes _ A K^\bullet ) \longrightarrow \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits ^\bullet (\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits ^\bullet (K^\bullet , A), A)  \]
</div>
    <p>
       which up to sign uses the evaluation map in each degree, see Lemma <a href="/tag/0A60" data-tag="0A60">15.73.6</a>. (For sign rules see Section <a href="/tag/0FNG" data-tag="0FNG">15.74</a>.) Thus this map defines a canonical isomorphism $(K^\vee )^\vee \cong K$ as the double dual of a finite projective module is itself. 
    <p>
      The second equality follows from the first by Lemma <a href="/tag/0A65" data-tag="0A65">15.75.1</a> and Derived Categories, Lemma <a href="/tag/064B" data-tag="064B">13.19.8</a> as well as the definition of Ext groups, see Derived Categories, Section <a href="/tag/06XP" data-tag="06XP">13.27</a>. Let $L^\bullet $ be a complex of $A$-modules representing $L$. By Section <a href="/tag/0FNG" data-tag="0FNG">15.74</a> item (<a href="/tag/0FNL" data-tag="0FNL">11</a>) there is a canonical isomorphism 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \text{Tot}(L^\bullet \otimes _ A E^\bullet ) \longrightarrow \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits ^\bullet (K^\bullet , L^\bullet )  \]
</div>
    <p>
       of complexes of $A$-modules (this uses Lemma <a href="/tag/0FNK" data-tag="0FNK">15.74.2</a> to see that $E^\bullet $ is the left dual of $K^\bullet $ in the category of complexes). This proves the first displayed equality and the proof is complete. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>